WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.977 --> 00:00:03.480
[Music starts] [Michael F. Chiang, MD]
There's never been a time in history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:08.692
where we've had so much access to science and&nbsp;
technology as being applied to health care.

00:00:08.692 --> 00:00:13.073
And I think one of the challenges for the&nbsp;
future is how to move things like biomedical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.073 --> 00:00:17.579
informatics and artificial intelligence and&nbsp;
data science into the mainstream of healthcare.

00:00:17.626 --> 00:00:20.940
[Dina Demner-Fushman, MD, PhD]
There is no one, other than NLM,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:27.550
who is specifically dedicated to&nbsp;
biomedical information retrieval.

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:34.787
We keep people focused on what really&nbsp;
matters for health and advancing medicine.

00:00:34.787 --> 00:00:39.300
[Zhiyong Lu, PhD]
Our research focuses on AI and machine learning.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:44.395
In the long run, what we really aim to do&nbsp;
is to teach computers to read and understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.395 --> 00:00:51.720
scientific papers like scientists, to interpret&nbsp;
x-rays or retinal images like radiologists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:56.791
at a speed and at a accuracy that's&nbsp;
above and beyond human ability.

00:00:56.791 --> 00:00:59.100
[Nigam H. Shah, MBBS, PhD]
Medicine has been dreaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:04.376
about having this ability to consult a&nbsp;
large library of patient records&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.418 --> 00:01:08.209
to make better decisions. We've been thinking&nbsp;
about this as a field for 40 years.

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:10.860
Finally, with the support of the&nbsp;
National Library of Medicine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:15.749
we have established its feasibility,&nbsp;
its safety, and long-term viability.

00:01:16.279 --> 00:01:18.073
[Sameer Antani, PhD]
Every clinician have seen a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.073 --> 00:01:22.926
number of patients in their clinical training&nbsp;
and they become very adept at that population.

00:01:23.042 --> 00:01:28.080
Machines, on the other hand, could be&nbsp;
trained on data that is free of bias from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:34.500
different parts of the world, from different&nbsp;
ethnicities, different age groups so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:39.064
there's an improved caregiving and therefore&nbsp;
a better expectation on treatment and care.

00:01:39.064 --> 00:01:41.600
[Alan McMillan, PhD]
What we've started to notice is that AI can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.860
be somewhat fragile. If it's given an unexpected&nbsp;
input, it can give a very unexpected output.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:51.105
And so really, the whole goal of&nbsp;
our research is to understand how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.105 --> 00:01:54.660
can we investigate where AI is&nbsp;
fragile. How can we essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:58.368
break it so that we can understand&nbsp;
how to fix it and make it stronger.

00:01:58.368 --> 00:02:03.039
But really, it comes down to the ability&nbsp;
to take what is theoretical research and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.039 --> 00:02:08.270
apply it to health care and improve the&nbsp;
treatment that we can provide patients.

00:02:09.342 --> 00:02:11.662
[Music fades to Silence]

